## Binary Search on Answer Space

### The Pattern
Whenever a problem asks for the **minimum possible maximum** or **maximum possible minimum** with a monotonic condition, apply Binary Search on the **answer range** rather than array indices.

```java
public int minEatingSpeed(int[] piles, int h) {
    int low = 1, high = Arrays.stream(piles).max().getAsInt(), ans = high;
    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
        if (canEat(piles, mid, h)) { ans = mid; high = mid - 1; }
        else low = mid + 1;
    }
    return ans;
}
private boolean canEat(int[] piles, int speed, int h) {
    long hours = 0;
    for (int p : piles) hours += (p + speed - 1) / speed;
    return hours <= h;
}
```

**Complexity:** Time O(N log(max)), Space O(1)

### Common Problems
- Koko Eating Bananas (LeetCode 875)
- Minimum Days to Make Bouquets (LeetCode 1482)
- Find the Smallest Divisor (LeetCode 1283)
- Split Array Largest Sum (LeetCode 410)

### Template
```java
int lo = minPossible, hi = maxPossible;
while (lo < hi) {
    int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    if (condition(mid)) hi = mid;   // feasible, try smaller
    else lo = mid + 1;              // not feasible, go larger
}
return lo;
```

## Sliding Window: Dynamic Window

```java
public int lengthOfLongestSubstring(String s) {
    int[] lastSeen = new int[128];
    Arrays.fill(lastSeen, -1);
    int maxLen = 0, left = 0;
    for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {
        char c = s.charAt(right);
        if (lastSeen[c] >= left) left = lastSeen[c] + 1;
        lastSeen[c] = right;
        maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, right - left + 1);
    }
    return maxLen;
}
```

**Complexity:** Time O(N), Space O(1)

### Fixed vs Variable Window
- **Fixed window** (size k): move both pointers together
- **Variable window**: expand right, shrink left when constraint violated

### Common Variable Window Problems
- Minimum Window Substring (LeetCode 76)
- Longest Repeating Character Replacement (LeetCode 424)
- Fruit Into Baskets (LeetCode 904)

## Topological Sort

### Kahn's Algorithm (BFS + In-Degree)
```java
public int[] findOrder(int n, int[][] prereqs) {
    List<List<Integer>> adj = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adj.add(new ArrayList<>());
    int[] inDeg = new int[n];
    for (int[] p : prereqs) { adj.get(p[1]).add(p[0]); inDeg[p[0]]++; }
    Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (inDeg[i] == 0) q.offer(i);
    int[] order = new int[n]; int idx = 0;
    while (!q.isEmpty()) {
        int cur = q.poll(); order[idx++] = cur;
        for (int nb : adj.get(cur)) if (--inDeg[nb] == 0) q.offer(nb);
    }
    return idx == n ? order : new int[0]; // empty = cycle detected
}
```

**Complexity:** Time O(V+E), Space O(V+E)

### Cycle detection: if idx < n after BFS, a cycle exists.

### DFS Coloring Alternative
- WHITE (0) = unvisited, GRAY (1) = in-stack, BLACK (2) = done
- Back edge to GRAY node = cycle

## Dijkstra's Algorithm

```java
public int[] dijkstra(int n, List<List<int[]>> adj, int src) {
    int[] dist = new int[n];
    Arrays.fill(dist, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
    dist[src] = 0;
    PriorityQueue<int[]> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a[1]));
    pq.offer(new int[]{src, 0});
    while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
        int[] cur = pq.poll();
        int u = cur[0], d = cur[1];
        if (d > dist[u]) continue; // stale entry
        for (int[] e : adj.get(u)) {
            int v = e[0], w = e[1];
            if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
                dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
                pq.offer(new int[]{v, dist[v]});
            }
        }
    }
    return dist;
}
```

**Complexity:** Time O((V+E) log V), Space O(V+E)

### Algorithm Comparison
| Algorithm | Weights | Complexity |
|---|---|---|
| Dijkstra | Non-negative | O((V+E) log V) |
| Bellman-Ford | Any (detect neg cycle) | O(VE) |
| Floyd-Warshall | All pairs | O(V³) |

## 0/1 Knapsack

### Space-Optimized 1D Solution
```java
public int knapsack(int W, int[] wt, int[] val, int n) {
    int[] dp = new int[W + 1];
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        for (int c = W; c >= wt[i]; c--) // REVERSE — key for 0/1
            dp[c] = Math.max(dp[c], val[i] + dp[c - wt[i]]);
    return dp[W];
}
```

### 0/1 vs Unbounded Knapsack
- **0/1** (each item once): iterate capacity **backwards**
- **Unbounded** (reuse items, e.g. Coin Change): iterate capacity **forwards**

### Subset Sum
```java
public boolean canPartition(int[] nums) {
    int sum = Arrays.stream(nums).sum();
    if (sum % 2 != 0) return false;
    int target = sum / 2;
    boolean[] dp = new boolean[target + 1];
    dp[0] = true;
    for (int n : nums)
        for (int j = target; j >= n; j--)
            dp[j] |= dp[j - n];
    return dp[target];
}
```

**Complexity:** Time O(N×W), Space O(W)

## LCS & String DP

```java
public int longestCommonSubsequence(String s1, String s2) {
    int m = s1.length(), n = s2.length();
    int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
            dp[i][j] = s1.charAt(i-1) == s2.charAt(j-1)
                ? 1 + dp[i-1][j-1]
                : Math.max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]);
    return dp[m][n];
}
```

### Edit Distance (Levenshtein)
```java
public int minDistance(String w1, String w2) {
    int m = w1.length(), n = w2.length();
    int[][] dp = new int[m+1][n+1];
    for (int i = 0; i <= m; i++) dp[i][0] = i;
    for (int j = 0; j <= n; j++) dp[0][j] = j;
    for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++)
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
            dp[i][j] = w1.charAt(i-1) == w2.charAt(j-1)
                ? dp[i-1][j-1]
                : 1 + Math.min(dp[i-1][j-1], Math.min(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]));
    return dp[m][n];
}
```

**Complexity:** Time O(m×n), Space O(m×n) → O(min(m,n)) with rolling rows

## Two Pointers Pattern

### Container With Most Water
```java
public int maxArea(int[] h) {
    int l = 0, r = h.length - 1, max = 0;
    while (l < r) {
        max = Math.max(max, Math.min(h[l], h[r]) * (r - l));
        if (h[l] < h[r]) l++; else r--;
    }
    return max;
}
```

### 3Sum (sorted + two pointers per anchor)
```java
public List<List<Integer>> threeSum(int[] nums) {
    Arrays.sort(nums);
    List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length - 2; i++) {
        if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i-1]) continue; // skip dups
        int l = i + 1, r = nums.length - 1;
        while (l < r) {
            int sum = nums[i] + nums[l] + nums[r];
            if (sum == 0) {
                res.add(Arrays.asList(nums[i], nums[l], nums[r]));
                while (l < r && nums[l] == nums[l+1]) l++;
                while (l < r && nums[r] == nums[r-1]) r--;
                l++; r--;
            } else if (sum < 0) l++;
            else r--;
        }
    }
    return res;
}
```

**Complexity:** Time O(n log n + n²) for 3Sum, O(n) for Container. Space O(1).

## Fast & Slow Pointers

### Detect Cycle in Linked List
```java
public boolean hasCycle(ListNode head) {
    ListNode slow = head, fast = head;
    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
        slow = slow.next;
        fast = fast.next.next;
        if (slow == fast) return true;
    }
    return false;
}
```

### Find Cycle Start (Floyd Phase 2)
```java
public ListNode detectCycle(ListNode head) {
    ListNode slow = head, fast = head;
    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
        slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next.next;
        if (slow == fast) {
            // Phase 2: move one pointer to head
            ListNode ptr = head;
            while (ptr != slow) { ptr = ptr.next; slow = slow.next; }
            return ptr; // cycle start
        }
    }
    return null;
}
```

### Find Middle of Linked List
```java
public ListNode findMiddle(ListNode head) {
    ListNode slow = head, fast = head;
    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
        slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next.next;
    }
    return slow; // for even length: returns second middle
}
```

### Happy Number (cycle in sequence)
```java
public boolean isHappy(int n) {
    int slow = n, fast = sumOfSquares(n);
    while (fast != 1 && slow != fast) {
        slow = sumOfSquares(slow);
        fast = sumOfSquares(sumOfSquares(fast));
    }
    return fast == 1;
}
```

## Core Linked List Operations

### Reverse a Linked List (Iterative)
```java
public ListNode reverseList(ListNode head) {
    ListNode prev = null, cur = head;
    while (cur != null) {
        ListNode next = cur.next;
        cur.next = prev;
        prev = cur;
        cur = next;
    }
    return prev;
}
```

### Merge Two Sorted Lists
```java
public ListNode mergeTwoLists(ListNode l1, ListNode l2) {
    ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0), cur = dummy;
    while (l1 != null && l2 != null) {
        if (l1.val <= l2.val) { cur.next = l1; l1 = l1.next; }
        else { cur.next = l2; l2 = l2.next; }
        cur = cur.next;
    }
    cur.next = l1 != null ? l1 : l2;
    return dummy.next;
}
```

### Recursive Reverse
```java
public ListNode reverseList(ListNode head) {
    if (head == null || head.next == null) return head;
    ListNode newHead = reverseList(head.next);
    head.next.next = head;
    head.next = null;
    return newHead;
}
```

**Complexity:** All O(n) time, O(1) space (iterative) or O(n) space (recursive stack).

### Dummy Node Pattern
Always use a dummy head node for merge/delete operations — eliminates special-casing the head.

## Advanced Linked List Patterns

### Remove Nth Node from End (One Pass)
```java
public ListNode removeNthFromEnd(ListNode head, int n) {
    ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
    dummy.next = head;
    ListNode fast = dummy, slow = dummy;
    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) fast = fast.next; // advance fast by n+1
    while (fast != null) { fast = fast.next; slow = slow.next; }
    slow.next = slow.next.next; // remove node
    return dummy.next;
}
```

### Palindrome Linked List (O(1) space)
```java
public boolean isPalindrome(ListNode head) {
    // 1. Find middle
    ListNode slow = head, fast = head;
    while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
        slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next.next;
    }
    // 2. Reverse second half
    ListNode rev = reverse(slow);
    // 3. Compare
    ListNode p1 = head, p2 = rev;
    while (p2 != null) {
        if (p1.val != p2.val) return false;
        p1 = p1.next; p2 = p2.next;
    }
    return true;
}
```

### One-pass trick: Lead pointer n steps ahead, then move both until lead hits null — slow is now at the node before the target.

## Stack Fundamentals

### Valid Parentheses
```java
public boolean isValid(String s) {
    Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
        if (c == '(') stack.push(')');
        else if (c == '[') stack.push(']');
        else if (c == '{') stack.push('}');
        else if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != c) return false;
    }
    return stack.isEmpty();
}
```

### Min Stack (O(1) getMin)
```java
class MinStack {
    Deque<int[]> stack = new ArrayDeque<>(); // [val, currentMin]
    public void push(int val) {
        int min = stack.isEmpty() ? val : Math.min(val, stack.peek()[1]);
        stack.push(new int[]{val, min});
    }
    public void pop() { stack.pop(); }
    public int top() { return stack.peek()[0]; }
    public int getMin() { return stack.peek()[1]; }
}
```

### Daily Temperatures (Monotonic Stack intro)
```java
public int[] dailyTemperatures(int[] temps) {
    int n = temps.length;
    int[] res = new int[n];
    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>(); // indices
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        while (!stack.isEmpty() && temps[i] > temps[stack.peek()])
            res[stack.peek()] = i - stack.pop();
        stack.push(i);
    }
    return res;
}
```

**Use ArrayDeque, never Stack class** — Stack extends Vector which is synchronized.

## Monotonic Stack

A stack where elements are kept in monotone order. Pop when incoming element violates the order — that's when you process the result.

### Next Greater Element
```java
public int[] nextGreaterElements(int[] nums) {
    int n = nums.length;
    int[] res = new int[n];
    Arrays.fill(res, -1);
    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < 2 * n; i++) { // circular: iterate twice
        while (!stack.isEmpty() && nums[i % n] > nums[stack.peek()])
            res[stack.pop()] = nums[i % n];
        if (i < n) stack.push(i);
    }
    return res;
}
```

### Largest Rectangle in Histogram
```java
public int largestRectangleArea(int[] heights) {
    Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
    int max = 0, n = heights.length;
    for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
        int h = i == n ? 0 : heights[i];
        while (!stack.isEmpty() && h < heights[stack.peek()]) {
            int height = heights[stack.pop()];
            int width = stack.isEmpty() ? i : i - stack.peek() - 1;
            max = Math.max(max, height * width);
        }
        stack.push(i);
    }
    return max;
}
```

### When to use:
- **Monotone decreasing stack**: next greater / largest rectangle
- **Monotone increasing stack**: next smaller / trapping rain water

## Sliding Window Maximum

### Monotonic Deque Solution
```java
public int[] maxSlidingWindow(int[] nums, int k) {
    int n = nums.length;
    int[] res = new int[n - k + 1];
    Deque<Integer> dq = new ArrayDeque<>(); // stores indices, decreasing values
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        // Remove indices out of window
        while (!dq.isEmpty() && dq.peekFirst() < i - k + 1)
            dq.pollFirst();
        // Remove smaller elements from back (they'll never be max)
        while (!dq.isEmpty() && nums[dq.peekLast()] < nums[i])
            dq.pollLast();
        dq.offerLast(i);
        if (i >= k - 1) res[i - k + 1] = nums[dq.peekFirst()];
    }
    return res;
}
```

**Complexity:** Time O(n), Space O(k)

### BFS Template (Level Order)
```java
public List<List<Integer>> levelOrder(TreeNode root) {
    List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
    if (root == null) return res;
    Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();
    q.offer(root);
    while (!q.isEmpty()) {
        int size = q.size();
        List<Integer> level = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            TreeNode node = q.poll();
            level.add(node.val);
            if (node.left != null) q.offer(node.left);
            if (node.right != null) q.offer(node.right);
        }
        res.add(level);
    }
    return res;
}
```

## Binary Tree Traversals

### DFS Traversals (Recursive — clean, simple)
```java
void inorder(TreeNode root, List<Integer> res) {
    if (root == null) return;
    inorder(root.left, res);
    res.add(root.val);        // ← LEFT → ROOT → RIGHT
    inorder(root.right, res);
}
void preorder(TreeNode root, List<Integer> res) {
    if (root == null) return;
    res.add(root.val);        // ← ROOT → LEFT → RIGHT
    preorder(root.left, res);
    preorder(root.right, res);
}
```

### Iterative Inorder (Interview favorite)
```java
public List<Integer> inorderIterative(TreeNode root) {
    List<Integer> res = new ArrayList<>();
    Deque<TreeNode> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
    TreeNode cur = root;
    while (cur != null || !stack.isEmpty()) {
        while (cur != null) { stack.push(cur); cur = cur.left; }
        cur = stack.pop();
        res.add(cur.val);
        cur = cur.right;
    }
    return res;
}
```

### Traversal Order Cheat Sheet
| Traversal | Order | Use Case |
|---|---|---|
| Inorder | L → Root → R | BST sorted output |
| Preorder | Root → L → R | Serialize tree |
| Postorder | L → R → Root | Delete tree, evaluate expression |
| Level Order | BFS | Shortest path, level stats |

## Key Tree DFS Patterns

### Max Depth
```java
public int maxDepth(TreeNode root) {
    if (root == null) return 0;
    return 1 + Math.max(maxDepth(root.left), maxDepth(root.right));
}
```

### Diameter (longest path between any two nodes)
```java
int diameter = 0;
public int diameterOfBinaryTree(TreeNode root) {
    depth(root); return diameter;
}
private int depth(TreeNode node) {
    if (node == null) return 0;
    int l = depth(node.left), r = depth(node.right);
    diameter = Math.max(diameter, l + r); // path through this node
    return 1 + Math.max(l, r);
}
```

### Path Sum II (all root-to-leaf paths)
```java
public List<List<Integer>> pathSum(TreeNode root, int target) {
    List<List<Integer>> res = new ArrayList<>();
    dfs(root, target, new ArrayList<>(), res);
    return res;
}
void dfs(TreeNode node, int rem, List<Integer> path, List<List<Integer>> res) {
    if (node == null) return;
    path.add(node.val);
    if (node.left == null && node.right == null && rem == node.val)
        res.add(new ArrayList<>(path));
    dfs(node.left, rem - node.val, path, res);
    dfs(node.right, rem - node.val, path, res);
    path.remove(path.size() - 1); // backtrack
}
```

### LCA (Lowest Common Ancestor)
```java
public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
    if (root == null || root == p || root == q) return root;
    TreeNode left = lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, p, q);
    TreeNode right = lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, p, q);
    return left != null && right != null ? root : (left != null ? left : right);
}
```

## BST Key Properties

BST Invariant: left < root < right (all descendants, not just immediate children)

### Validate BST (with range bounds)
```java
public boolean isValidBST(TreeNode root) {
    return validate(root, Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE);
}
private boolean validate(TreeNode node, long min, long max) {
    if (node == null) return true;
    if (node.val <= min || node.val >= max) return false;
    return validate(node.left, min, node.val) &&
           validate(node.right, node.val, max);
}
```

### Kth Smallest (inorder = sorted)
```java
int count = 0, result = 0;
public int kthSmallest(TreeNode root, int k) {
    inorder(root, k); return result;
}
void inorder(TreeNode node, int k) {
    if (node == null) return;
    inorder(node.left, k);
    if (++count == k) { result = node.val; return; }
    inorder(node.right, k);
}
```

### BST LCA (use property — no full traversal needed)
```java
public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
    if (p.val < root.val && q.val < root.val) return lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, p, q);
    if (p.val > root.val && q.val > root.val) return lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, p, q);
    return root; // split point = LCA
}
```

**BST operations:** Search, Insert, Delete all O(h) — O(log n) balanced, O(n) skewed.

## Graph BFS Pattern

### BFS Template
```java
void bfs(int[][] grid, int startR, int startC) {
    int m = grid.length, n = grid[0].length;
    boolean[][] visited = new boolean[m][n];
    Queue<int[]> q = new LinkedList<>();
    q.offer(new int[]{startR, startC});
    visited[startR][startC] = true;
    int steps = 0;
    int[][] dirs = {{0,1},{0,-1},{1,0},{-1,0}};
    while (!q.isEmpty()) {
        int size = q.size();
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            int[] cell = q.poll();
            for (int[] d : dirs) {
                int nr = cell[0] + d[0], nc = cell[1] + d[1];
                if (nr >= 0 && nr < m && nc >= 0 && nc < n && !visited[nr][nc]) {
                    visited[nr][nc] = true;
                    q.offer(new int[]{nr, nc});
                }
            }
        }
        steps++;
    }
}
```

### Rotting Oranges (Multi-source BFS)
Start BFS from all rotten oranges simultaneously — all sources at level 0.

### Word Ladder (State BFS)
Each word = node. Change one letter at a time = edge. Use Set for O(1) lookup.

```java
// BFS from beginWord to endWord
Queue<String> q = new LinkedList<>();
Set<String> wordSet = new HashSet<>(wordList);
q.offer(beginWord); wordSet.remove(beginWord);
int steps = 1;
while (!q.isEmpty()) {
    for (int size = q.size(); size > 0; size--) {
        String word = q.poll();
        char[] ch = word.toCharArray();
        for (int i = 0; i < ch.length; i++) {
            char orig = ch[i];
            for (char c = 'a'; c <= 'z'; c++) {
                ch[i] = c;
                String next = new String(ch);
                if (next.equals(endWord)) return steps + 1;
                if (wordSet.remove(next)) q.offer(next);
            }
            ch[i] = orig;
        }
    }
    steps++;
}
```

## Union-Find (DSU)

### Implementation with Path Compression + Union by Rank
```java
class DSU {
    int[] parent, rank;
    int components;
    DSU(int n) {
        parent = new int[n]; rank = new int[n]; components = n;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
    }
    int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]); // path compression
        return parent[x];
    }
    boolean union(int x, int y) {
        int px = find(x), py = find(y);
        if (px == py) return false; // already same component — edge is redundant
        if (rank[px] < rank[py]) { int t = px; px = py; py = t; }
        parent[py] = px;
        if (rank[px] == rank[py]) rank[px]++;
        components--;
        return true;
    }
}
```

### Redundant Connection
```java
public int[] findRedundantConnection(int[][] edges) {
    DSU dsu = new DSU(edges.length + 1);
    for (int[] e : edges)
        if (!dsu.union(e[0], e[1])) return e; // already connected = cycle
    return new int[0];
}
```

### Number of Connected Components
```java
DSU dsu = new DSU(n);
for (int[] e : edges) dsu.union(e[0], e[1]);
return dsu.components;
```

**Complexity:** Nearly O(1) per operation — O(α(n)) amortized (inverse Ackermann function).

## Heap Patterns

### Kth Largest (min-heap of size K)
```java
public int findKthLargest(int[] nums, int k) {
    PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();
    for (int n : nums) {
        minHeap.offer(n);
        if (minHeap.size() > k) minHeap.poll(); // keep only top K
    }
    return minHeap.peek();
}
```
Time O(n log k), Space O(k)

### Top K Frequent Elements
```java
public int[] topKFrequent(int[] nums, int k) {
    Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
    for (int n : nums) freq.merge(n, 1, Integer::sum);
    PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap =
        new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(freq::get));
    for (int key : freq.keySet()) {
        minHeap.offer(key);
        if (minHeap.size() > k) minHeap.poll();
    }
    int[] res = new int[k];
    for (int i = k - 1; i >= 0; i--) res[i] = minHeap.poll();
    return res;
}
```

### Merge K Sorted Lists
```java
public ListNode mergeKLists(ListNode[] lists) {
    PriorityQueue<ListNode> pq =
        new PriorityQueue<>(Comparator.comparingInt(n -> n.val));
    for (ListNode l : lists) if (l != null) pq.offer(l);
    ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0), cur = dummy;
    while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
        cur.next = pq.poll();
        cur = cur.next;
        if (cur.next != null) pq.offer(cur.next);
    }
    return dummy.next;
}
```
Time O(n log k) where n = total nodes, k = number of lists

## Two Heaps Pattern

### Median of Data Stream
```java
class MedianFinder {
    // maxHeap: lower half, minHeap: upper half
    PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
    PriorityQueue<Integer> minHeap = new PriorityQueue<>();

    public void addNum(int num) {
        maxHeap.offer(num);
        // Balance: ensure maxHeap.top <= minHeap.top
        minHeap.offer(maxHeap.poll());
        // Balance sizes: maxHeap size == minHeap or maxHeap size == minHeap + 1
        if (maxHeap.size() < minHeap.size())
            maxHeap.offer(minHeap.poll());
    }

    public double findMedian() {
        if (maxHeap.size() > minHeap.size()) return maxHeap.peek();
        return (maxHeap.peek() + minHeap.peek()) / 2.0;
    }
}
```

**Complexity:** addNum O(log n), findMedian O(1)

### Invariant:
- maxHeap contains the SMALLER half
- minHeap contains the LARGER half
- maxHeap.size() == minHeap.size() or maxHeap.size() == minHeap.size() + 1
- maxHeap.peek() <= minHeap.peek() always

