## Recursion & Memoization

### Fibonacci: O(2^n) → O(n) with memo
```java
// Naive: O(2^n)
public int fib(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);
}

// Memoized: O(n) time, O(n) space
public int fib(int n, int[] memo) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    if (memo[n] != 0) return memo[n];
    memo[n] = fib(n-1, memo) + fib(n-2, memo);
    return memo[n];
}

// Bottom-up DP: O(n) time, O(1) space
public int fib(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    int a = 0, b = 1;
    for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) { int c = a + b; a = b; b = c; }
    return b;
}
```

### Recursion vs Iteration vs Memoization vs Tabulation
| Approach | Time | Space | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| Pure recursion | O(2^n) | O(n) stack | Redundant sub-problems |
| Memoization (top-down) | O(n) | O(n) memo + stack | Natural, intuitive |
| Tabulation (bottom-up) | O(n) | O(n) table | No stack overflow risk |
| Space-optimized DP | O(n) | O(1) | Best for production |

### When Recursion Fails
- Deep recursion → StackOverflowError (default Java stack ~500-1000 frames)
- Convert to iteration + explicit stack for large inputs

