## Coin Change & Unbounded Knapsack

### Coin Change (min coins)
```java
public int coinChange(int[] coins, int amount) {
    int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
    Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1); // infinity
    dp[0] = 0;
    for (int coin : coins)
        for (int j = coin; j <= amount; j++) // FORWARD = unbounded
            dp[j] = Math.min(dp[j], dp[j - coin] + 1);
    return dp[amount] > amount ? -1 : dp[amount];
}
```

### Coin Change II (number of combinations)
```java
public int change(int amount, int[] coins) {
    int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
    dp[0] = 1;
    for (int coin : coins) // outer loop = coins (avoid permutations)
        for (int j = coin; j <= amount; j++)
            dp[j] += dp[j - coin];
    return dp[amount];
}
```

### BFS Alternative for Coin Change
Coin Change can be solved with BFS (treat amount as target, each coin = edge weight 1). BFS guarantees minimum hops (coins).

### Combinations vs Permutations
- **Outer: coins, Inner: amount** → Combinations (each arrangement counted once)
- **Outer: amount, Inner: coins** → Permutations (order matters — different for [1,2] vs [2,1])

**Complexity:** Time O(amount × coins), Space O(amount)

## Longest Increasing Subsequence

### O(n²) DP
```java
public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
    int n = nums.length;
    int[] dp = new int[n];
    Arrays.fill(dp, 1);
    int max = 1;
    for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)
            if (nums[j] < nums[i]) dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1);
        max = Math.max(max, dp[i]);
    }
    return max;
}
```

### O(n log n) Patience Sorting
```java
public int lengthOfLIS(int[] nums) {
    List<Integer> tails = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int n : nums) {
        int lo = 0, hi = tails.size();
        while (lo < hi) { // binary search for insertion point
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (tails.get(mid) < n) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid;
        }
        if (lo == tails.size()) tails.add(n);
        else tails.set(lo, n);
    }
    return tails.size();
}
```

### Russian Doll Envelopes (2D LIS)
Sort by width ascending, height descending. Then LIS on height only.

```java
Arrays.sort(envelopes, (a, b) -> a[0] == b[0] ? b[1] - a[1] : a[0] - b[0]);
int[] heights = Arrays.stream(envelopes).mapToInt(e -> e[1]).toArray();
return lengthOfLIS(heights);
```

**Complexity:** O(n log n) patience sort, O(n) space. tails array is not the actual LIS — just its length.

